          ],
          "grounding_supports": [
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                0,
                1
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": null,
                "end_index": 153,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "When someone is sick\u2014especially when they are feeling nauseated or about to throw up\u2014it is common to say they look \"pale\" or \"green around the gills\""
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                0
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 157,
                "end_index": 238,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "While we don't actually turn the vibrant, lime-green color of a cartoon character"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                0,
                2
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 157,
                "end_index": 299,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "While we don't actually turn the vibrant, lime-green color of a cartoon character, human skin can take on a distinct, sickly greyish-green hue"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                3
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 458,
                "end_index": 629,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Why We Turn Pale: Vasoconstriction\nThe primary reason your face loses its color when you are sick is a process called **vasoconstriction**\u2014the narrowing of blood vessels"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                4,
                3
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 633,
                "end_index": 805,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "When you get a sudden wave of nausea, experience a fever, or go into shock, your autonomic nervous system (specifically the \"fight-or-flight\" response) kicks into high gear"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                4,
                5
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 891,
                "end_index": 1079,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "To protect you, it redirects blood flow away from non-essential areas (like the skin of your face and fingers) and sends it to your vital internal organs, like your heart, brain, and lungs"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                6,
                7
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1081,
                "end_index": 1225,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Losing the \"glow\":** Human skin gets its warm, pinkish-red undertone from oxygen-rich blood pumping through capillaries close to the surface"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                6,
                8
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1227,
                "end_index": 1341,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "When vasoconstriction drains that blood away, your face immediately loses its warm flush and becomes pale or white"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                9,
                6
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1356,
                "end_index": 1497,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Why We Look \"Green\": The Color-Mixing Illusion\nOnce the warm red tones of your blood vanish, your skin\u2019s natural undertones are left behind"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                10
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1499,
                "end_index": 1544,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "This creates an optical illusion of greenness"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                9,
                11
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1547,
                "end_index": 1691,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Yellow undertones:** Human skin naturally contains varying amounts of melanin and carotene, which provide yellow, olive, or brown undertones"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                9,
                10
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1694,
                "end_index": 1877,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Blue veins:** Your veins are not actually blue, but the way light scatters and refracts through your skin (called *Rayleigh scattering*) makes them appear bluish-purple to the eye"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                9,
                10
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1992,
                "end_index": 2147,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "But when vasoconstriction pulls the red blood away, you are left with only the **yellow** undertones of your skin and the **blue** reflection of your veins"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                9,
                8
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2208,
                "end_index": 2312,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "This creates a dull, greenish-grey hue, which is especially noticeable in people with olive or fair skin"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                12
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2327,
                "end_index": 2443,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Our Brains are Hardwired to See \"Sickly Green\"\nHuman beings are evolutionarily programmed to spot sickness in others"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                12
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2445,
                "end_index": 2497,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "This helped our ancestors avoid contagious pathogens"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                11
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2501,
                "end_index": 2645,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "A study by the National Eye Institute (NEI) found that the human brain's visual processing system is uniquely sensitive to changes in face color"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                11
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2647,
                "end_index": 2914,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "When someone loses their healthy red flush, our brains do not just register them as \"less red\"\u2014our visual system actively highlights the shift, making us perceive the person as having a \"sickly green\" complexion even if the physical color shift is relatively subtle"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                13,
                14
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2929,
                "end_index": 3136,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Historical Context: The Real \"Green Sickness\"\nHistorically, there was a recognized medical condition called **chlorosis**, or \"green sickness,\" which was highly documented from the 17th to the 19th centuries"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                13,
                14
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3138,
                "end_index": 3257,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "It primarily affected young women and was characterized by fatigue, weakness, and a distinct greenish-yellow skin color"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                13,
                15
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3260,
                "end_index": 3434,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "In the 1930s, doctors finally realized that chlorosis was not a mysterious, independent disease, but actually a severe form of **hypochromic anemia** (severe iron deficiency)"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                13
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3436,
                "end_index": 3605,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Because iron is essential for producing hemoglobin (the protein that makes red blood cells red), patients with this severe deficiency had incredibly pale red blood cells"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                13
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3607,
                "end_index": 3700,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "This lack of red pigment allowed their skin's yellow-green undertones to completely take over"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            }
          ],
          "retrieval_metadata": null,
